(project tuna-local
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: local - run sabra on a laptop with no server, then sync what it made to the main instance. Today the pure core runs offline but the language does not: the CLI compiles with no dictionary (std names are unbound), has no REPL, answers no prim, and prints results as ternary. Four offline moves (L1 stdlib in the CLI, L2 a local REPL over a file dictionary, L3 a local prim host that writes a deriv-shaped journal, L4 typed output) and two sync moves (L5 definitions become ops in the tree substrate, L6 a local instance is a federation peer). Research question, stated so it can be refuted: is local-first tuna just existing parts placed on a laptop - the pure core, the dictionary, the deriv record and the federation ops chain - so that sync needs no merge algorithm, no new route and no change to the calculus?")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 chapter authored (roerick + Claude). EVIDENCE, read in-tree this date: (1) `tuna compile` on `(not true)` exits 1 "unbound variable true" and on `(list-length [1 2 3])` exits 1 "unbound variable list-length": cli/bin/main.ml calls Bracket.compile_source with no ?dictionary, although Sexp.parse and compile_source already take one. The frozen stdlib is on disk (stdlib/v1/trees, manifest), but only server/lib/dune generates stdlib_embed.ml, which server/lib/stdlib_seed.ml seeds into Postgres as the sabralib identity's rows. (2) The REPL (server/lib/repl_cmd.ml, Repl_cmd.effective_dictionary) reads repl_dict, a mutable (identity, name) -> ternary table (migrations/0004-repl-dict.sql) with no history and no op log, so a definition has nothing to sync from. (3) cli --semantics already reaches every registered semantics through Accel.registry_of_semantics (fast1, fast2, fast3; `eval-compiled 0 0 --semantics fast2` answers normal), but deriv/lib/deriv.ml accepts only v0 and v1, so deriv-check cannot verify a fast* run offline; server replay (server/lib/replay.ml) can. (4) The pieces sync reuses already exist: content-addressed values (GET /api/fed/value/:hash, rehash on receipt), the hash-chained tree_ops log (migrations/0005-tree-paths.sql), federation's ops windows applied into ns/<peer>/ with inline tree and bytes values (borg/federation.borg, server/lib/api.ml fed_apply_core), and deriv records (borg/deriv.borg). Federation's conflict law v0 governs sync: append-only, no merge, a re-apply SHADOWS, divergence surfaces as two namespaces. OWNER DIRECTION carried from eval-modes (2026-11-25): keep everything runnable offline, with no first-class dependency on a server to record or check a run. OWNER DECISION (2026-11-25, after review): a conflicting definition surfaces as two namespaces under federation's existing law; no conflict-refusing apply mode. PRECEDENT: pp/borge/local-first.borg (a local lens is a branch of the one tree, synced by three-way tree-diff; sync-conflicts still planned there). Tuna differs because its values are immutable hashes: only the name layer can diverge.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 REVISED after independent review (Fable, request changes). Findings taken: L5/L6 had assumed apply refuses a stale write - it shadows, so conflict is now a comparison of two namespaces; L6 had pushed deriv records to a server route that does not exist - records now travel as bytes values through apply and are checked by whoever reads them; L5 keyed conflict on prev_version, which is instance-local - it now keys on the previous value hash; L3 now names the deriv-check semantics gap as its own step; L1 names the frozen manifest as the std reference; L6 names operator peer enrolment and where the push prompt lives; evidence (3) corrected (the CLI already accepts fast2/fast3).|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 SECOND REVIEW (Fable, approve with changes) taken: the L2 log is now tree_ops-shaped so it pushes as an ops window, with prev_hash local-only; undef is a put of the zero-length bytes marker, never delete; dict/ is reserved from tree grants; the runs/<run-id> put op and the over-cap record case are written down; the ops-window read exposure is an OPEN owner decision that gates L5.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 OWNER DECISION on read exposure: dictionaries are private by default with a per-identity public option ("honestly i don't see why it would not be private but maybe somebody wants that"). Recorded in L6 as an ownership read filter on the ops window, a known LO2 instance that federation's chapter must specify before L5 lands.|) *)
 (section local
  (doc "Six moves. L1-L4 are offline-only and each is useful alone; L5-L6 add sync. None may change the calculus, the IR, the step count or the prim set, and sync adds no route; the local tools reuse the server's row shapes so their output can be synced later without conversion.")
  (status planned)
  (subsection l1-stdlib-offline
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 L1 IMPLEMENTED (branch tuna-local-l1; merged after review). SPEC EDITS, owner to confirm: the source-level command is eval-source (tuna eval is the differential harness's corpus command), arg terms are source (a raw tree is %<ternary>), and the server does not link tuna_stdlib - a second embed from stdlib/v1/trees, held equal to the server's seeded core.defs by tests/local_tests.ml. CODE: stdlib/lib (library tuna_stdlib: Std_trees generated from stdlib/v1/trees by tools/stdprobe/embed_dict.py, Std_dict.dictionary) - the server's stdlib_embed (core.defs source, seeded through def rounds) is untouched, and so is the accelerators' Accel_trees embed. cli/bin/main.ml: compile resolves against Std_dict and prints a lock line per resolved std name (name sha256); NEW eval-source <file> [arg-terms...] compiles program and each arg term against std (one lock over program and args), then runs with the shared --fuel/--cap/--semantics flags (eval-compiled's flag parsing factored into parse_run_flags; the existing `tuna eval <corpus-file>` is the differential harness's, so the source-level command is eval-source, not eval). browser/bin passes the same dictionary to tunaEval. No reader builtin is a std name (pair/cons), so a source with no free names compiles exactly as before. EVIDENCE: dune build --root . @all exit 0; dune runtest --root . exit 0 (store suites skip silently with PG down); tests/local_tests.ml 3/3 - every frozen row equals the manifest (name, order, sha256, size) and round-trips, every frozen tree equals what Tuna_server.Stdlib_embed.defs compile to in seeding order, resolution order, shadowing, pins and unbound errors; scripts/verify-differential.sh 103/103; on an isolated dev instance (port 18190, fresh data dir) scripts/verify-8-stdlib.sh green (seeded = manifest) and scripts/verify-18-local.sh ACCEPT 18 OK - 21/21 sources (8 probes incl. a holding and a moved pin, + every scripts/*/*.sabra) give the same program hash and lock through the CLI as POST /api/programs for a caller with no defs, and the two both-refused sources (the moved pin; todo-v02-board.sabra, whose key-total is a probe-identity def) carry the same compile message on both sides. tests/cli_local.sh (runtest) drives the CLI binary: lock lines, holding and moved pin, eval-source with source, %ternary and std-name arg terms. Manual: `tuna compile` on (not true) -> leaf, 2 steps, lock not+true; node tunaEval on (list-length [1 2 3]) -> normal 1110.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The stdlib dictionary reaches every local front end through a
      small library (tuna_stdlib) the CLI and the browser build link,
      generated from the frozen stdlib/v1/trees. The server keeps its
      own embed of core.defs, which it seeds through def rounds; the
      two embeds are held equal by a test that compiles core.defs in
      seeding order and compares every tree to the frozen row. The reference
      for std is the frozen manifest (stdlib/v1/manifest), not any
      instance's database: an instance whose sabralib rows differ from
      the manifest is LO1 drift on that instance, not a CLI bug.

      `tuna compile` and a new source-level `tuna eval-source` (compile,
      then run on arg terms; a raw tree is written %<ternary>) resolve
      names as the
      server does for an identity with no defs: lambda param > std >
      reader builtins. Pins (tool-language T2) and the compile lock
      work unchanged. `--semantics` keeps taking every registered
      semantics through the registry the server uses.

      ACCEPTANCE: (not true) and (list-length [1 2 3]) compile through
      the CLI; every corpus source compiles to the same hash in the CLI
      as POST /api/programs answers for an identity with no defs on an
      instance seeded from the manifest; a moved pin is the same
      compile error offline and online. |))))
  (subsection l2-local-repl
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 L2 IMPLEMENTED (branch tuna-local-l2; merged after review). CODE: repl/lib (library tuna_repl, pure): Repl_core holds the command grammar, parse_command, lock_of and compile_with_dict moved byte-for-byte out of server/lib/repl_cmd.ml, which now includes it (Tuna_server.Repl_cmd names unchanged); Patch moved from server/lib, which re-exports it. local/lib (library tuna_local): Local_store (identity file; values/<sha256>, a non-hash name never read as a path; ops.jsonl rows = federation window rows plus local prev_value_hash, chained by a copy of Store.op_concat; last = result hash or "-" for a resultless round; a lockf'd lock held for each round; appends are one write + fsync, an uncommitted final fragment is ignored and truncated by the next append) and Local_repl (rounds as Repl_cmd.execute: eval/def compile against own defs over the frozen std; eval runs on the round's inputs and fuel, def on none with the server's fixed 1e6; every eval/def round becomes the last round, with or without a result; undef puts the zero-length-bytes marker; get/patch/first-diff/dict structural; every failure an error value). cli: tuna repl [--dir D] [--identity N] [--input TERM]... [--fuel N] [--cap N] [--semantics S], one command per stdin line, the server REPL's 1e6 defaults, exit 1 if any round failed, exit 2 on a bad flag or directory. A directory holds one identity (fixed at creation). Prim calls are not answered locally yet (L3). Known, documented differences that no round's answer shows (local_repl.ml header): get/first-diff by hash read the local value store, compile is untimed, --input is a source term, dict is byte-ordered, error texts carry no server run id. REVIEW (Fable, request changes) taken: rows now parse with Api.parse_tree_op_json and walk with Store.verify_window_rows (ints, window prev_hash; the local binding field renamed prev_value_hash here and in L5/L6); resultless rounds move last; lock, fsync and torn-line handling; hash validation; def fuel; CLI flag values; 18.2 runs as a minted identity with exact error equality. EVIDENCE: dune build --root . @all exit 0; dune runtest --root . exit 0 with PG up on an isolated instance (1005 OK, 0 FAIL); tests/local_tests.ml l2 suite 5/5 (op chain formula = Store.op_concat; chain, versions, fold, undef marker, redef after undef names the marker, one identity per directory, tamper; log rows through the server's window parser and chain walk; torn final line and corrupt committed line; rounds incl. a fuel-starved round then get, and a path-like hash); scripts/verify-18-local.sh ACCEPT 18 OK as a fresh identity - 18.1 21/21, 18.2 20/20 rounds (def, eval with and without inputs incl. 517 steps, a fuel-5 round, get on last and by hash, a successful patch, first-diff, a def shadowing std not, undef, an unbound name, an unknown command, dict) with the same kind, status, result hash, steps, lock, dict rows and error text through tuna repl and /api/repl. The server REPL refactor re-verified live: verify-8, -12, -15 (and earlier -1, -3, -16) exit 0.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      `tuna repl` runs the REPL command grammar (eval, def, undef, get,
      patch, dict, first-diff; a bare term evaluates) against a local
      directory, default $TUNA_LOCAL_DIR, else $XDG_DATA_HOME/tuna,
      else ~/.local/share/tuna, never a database. Values
      live in a content-addressed directory (one file per tree, named
      by its sha256). The grammar and resolution order are shared code,
      not a copy: Repl_cmd's parse and dictionary assembly are factored
      away from the store so server and CLI call one implementation.

      The local store keeps one append-only op log whose rows are
      federation WINDOW rows from the start (the JSON of GET
      /api/fed/ops: every field Store.tree_op_concat hashes - seq, path,
      op, value_hash, prev_version, version, actor, ts, as the window's
      types - plus op_hash and prev_hash, the previous row's op_hash;
      chained as Store computes it, genesis 64 zeros), so L6 can push it
      with no conversion. A def is a put at dict/<identity>/<name>; an
      undef is a put of the undef marker (L5). Each dict row also
      carries a LOCAL-ONLY prev_value_hash (the value hash the name
      held before, the undef marker included), outside the chained
      fields: apply ignores it, so it serves the laptop's own
      divergence report. The current dictionary is the fold of the
      log. Hashes, not versions, because only hashes mean the same
      thing on two instances.

      ACCEPTANCE: a transcript of def/eval/undef rounds gives the same
      result hashes and step counts locally and through /api/repl for
      a fresh identity. |))))
  (subsection l3-local-prims
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-26 L3 IMPLEMENTED (branch tuna-local-l3-repl; merged after independent review, APPROVE, and a delta review APPROVE). OWNER CONFIRMATIONS taken before landing: grants name prims only (--grant PRIM; '*' for any) because a store key is not a path, so the chapter's store/get:<path-prefix> form is dropped; the math family (math/add, math/sub, math/mul, math/cmp, math/neg) is in the local set and is now listed; repl eval and def rounds answer prims under the same grants as tuna run, seal no record, and L2's op-log shape is unchanged. CODE: prims/lib (library tuna_prims: Prim_core is the server's registry, handlers and contract moved out of server/lib/prims.ml, which re-exports; Math_prims moved out of server/lib/math_prims.ml, which re-exports); local/lib/local_run.ml (Flat_drive host with the local prim set, grants checked per call, the server's denial text as a data answer, http/get behind --allow-host and inside an answer-not-raise boundary, journal rows in Store.row_hash shape from genesis 64 zeros; execute seals D.build by hash with a runs/<run-id> put op; eval_run is the unsealed half the repl uses); local/lib/local_repl.ml (eval/def rounds run through eval_run, so a prim answers in the repl); cli/bin/main.ml (tuna run and tuna repl take --grant/--allow-host; repl validates --semantics); deriv/lib/deriv.ml (fast1-fast3 through Accel.registry_of_semantics, the same function server replay uses). REVIEW: independent review of f513ae6b APPROVE - A1 a v0 and a v1 run's records deriv-check VERIFIED (exit 0), A2 a flipped journal answer exit 1 (and a full reseal still caught by replay identity), A3 Replay.execute_fed reproduces status, result hash and step count, A4 the same for fast1-fast3 with the accelerator firing (1 step vs 1177); delta review of the repl change APPROVE. FINDINGS TAKEN: http/get resolution and socket errors now answer instead of raising; a fast* run with a prim is verified offline and server-replayed; the repl comments no longer claim a sealed record. FINDINGS RECORDED, NOT FIXED: Local_run and deriv.ml re-implement Run.mode_of_semantics, and Journal.row_fingerprint duplicates Store.row_hash with no cross-module pin - drift risks, no current defect. EVIDENCE: dune build --root=tuna exit 0; tests/local_tests.exe 12/12 (l3 suite 4/4: record verifies/replays/tamper; grants are arguments; repl prims incl. a def-then-eval call, with zero run rows; v1 and fast1-3 with and without a prim); tests/cli_local.sh ok (run record verify and tamper; repl --grant writes and an ungranted prim does not). On the pre-repl tree f513ae6b, dune runtest --root=tuna with PG up 1008 OK 0 FAIL, the differential harness 103/103, and verify-1..6, 9, 11, 16 green (recorded by the prior session). UNRUN HERE: the 1008-test store suite and scripts/verify-18-local.sh live (Postgres and the dev server are stopped); live A3 replay is covered only in-process (Replay.execute_fed).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A local run host drives the pure step core (Flat_drive, as the
      server does) and answers prims from a local registry: echo, now,
      uuid, store/get and store/put over the local directory, http/get
      only behind an explicit allowlist flag, and the math family
      (math/add, math/sub, math/mul, math/cmp, math/neg). Grants are
      command-line arguments and name prims: --grant echo, --grant
      math/add, or --grant '*' for any. A grant is checked at every
      prim call like the server's grant rows; a store key is not a
      path, so a grant carries no path prefix. A missing grant is a
      journaled error answer, never an exception. `tuna run` writes a
      journal with the server's row fields and hash chain
      (Store.row_hash encoding, genesis 64 zeros) and emits a sealed
      deriv record.

      `tuna repl` answers prims the same way in its eval and def
      rounds, under the same --grant and --allow-host flags, so an
      offline session can use a prim without leaving the REPL. A repl
      round is not a sealed run: L2's op-log shape (one row per
      dictionary effect) is unchanged.

      SEMANTICS GAP, RESOLVED IN THIS MOVE: deriv-check accepted only
      v0 and v1, while local runs may use fast1, fast2 or fast3.
      deriv/lib now resolves accelerators through
      Accel.registry_of_semantics - the same function server replay
      uses - so deriv-check verifies all five offline.

      ACCEPTANCE: a local v0 or v1 run with prims produces a deriv
      record deriv-check verifies (exit 0); flipping one journal answer
      makes it exit 1; the same program and journal answers replayed on
      the server reproduce the result hash and step count. The same
      holds for fast1, fast2 and fast3. A repl eval round answers a
      granted prim and answers the server's denial text for an
      ungranted one, with no record sealed. |))))
  (subsection l4-typed-output
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-26 L4 IMPLEMENTED AS THE FIRST FACTORY LEAF (execution brg_a130a6ef, branch factory/spec-tuna-borg-local-local-l4-1126-brg_a1, merged 9a2a6ad1; independent code review PASS). CODE: a new library tuna/codec/lib (Tuna_codec) holds json_codec.ml and the jay_values.ml it depends on, moved out of server/lib; server/lib/json_codec.ml and server/lib/jay_values.ml are now `include Tuna_codec.*` shims, so Tuna_server's names and POST /api/tool/run are unchanged (cli/bin/dune and server/lib/dune both link tuna_codec: one grammar, no second one); cli/bin/main.ml takes --in TYPE (repeatable, one JSON value per type, in order) and --out TYPE through that codec, prints `result <json>` and the ternary line, keeps pure ternary without --out, and exits 2 naming `--in: $.exit` on a mistyped value; cli_local.sh pins pass/fail/unverified, the ternary default and that error path, and tests/dune passes it scripts/gate-receipts/verdict.sabra. REVIEW: the factory's code review ran `dune runtest` in its own worktree (exit 0; cli_local ok; the 44-test codec suite green) and found no contradiction; the merge gate re-ran build (rc=0, 57s) and the claim gate (rc=0, 11s). EVIDENCE: the gate verdict through the CLI with the JSON observation answers `result "pass"`, `"fail"` and `"unverified"` - the same JSON verify-15's live 15.5 pins for POST /api/tool/run (three runs, replay verified). The equality is pinned structurally (one shared codec library) plus those two halves; no single command queried both sides in one run. FINDINGS TAKEN: the first review FAIL was a one-line mismatch (`--in.exit` emitted, `--in: $.exit` required); repaired in c0c60608, and the claim gate now runs cli_local.sh as well as the codec suite so an implement pass cannot report tests=ok with that check red. NOTE FOR LATER LEAVES: the implement pass stopped on its input-token cap after finishing the work (2M cumulative, mostly cache reads), not on cost ($0.39); the cap is a token budget for long exploration, not a spend signal.|) *)
   (execution
    (scope l4)
    (kind code))
   (details
    (doc (|
      Results decode through a declared type instead of printing as
      ternary. The tool-language T4 codec (server/lib/json_codec.ml)
      moves from the server rim into a library the CLI links; `--in
      TYPE` and `--out TYPE` take the same type grammar as POST
      /api/tool/run, written as one JSON value on the command line (for
      example --out '{"record":{"count":[1,"int"]}}'), so the CLI
      adds no second grammar. Without a type the CLI keeps printing
      ternary, since trees are untyped and a guess would be wrong (leaf
      is false, nil and zero at once).

      ACCEPTANCE: the gate verdict run through the CLI with a JSON
      observation answers the same JSON as /api/tool/run.
      |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "L4: the gate verdict through the CLI with a JSON observation answers the same JSON as /api/tool/run"))
  )
  (subsection l5-defs-as-ops
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-27 IMPLEMENTED (execution brg_eefa8909, branch factory/spec-tuna-borg-local-local-l5-1126-brg_ee, merged 95962294; independent code review PASS). CODE: store/lib/store.ml dict_path_of/dict_op_put append exactly one put row at dict/<identity>/<name> (version = ops seen at that exact path + 1) and refresh the derived repl_dict row; dict_op_undef puts the zero-length-bytes marker byte value and then a put citing it (never a delete, so apply replicates it); dict_fold folds the dict rows up to to_seq, last row wins and the marker drops the name; dict_backfill runs at boot, skips names the fold already holds and never rewrites repl_dict. server/lib/repl_cmd.ml do_def/do_undef route through those ops; server/lib/tree_prims.ml reaches_dict (None, "", "d", "dict/...", "dict/x" all reach) and api.ml grant_mint_core refuses a tree grant whose prefix reaches dict/, so a prim run cannot rewrite a dictionary. TESTS: fed 21/21 (new defs-as-ops group: one put per def, no delete on undef, past fold, backfill unchanged + idempotent, private pull omitted then served once public, grant refusal for None/dict/l5DefA//d), store 18/18, repl 4/4, local 13/13. REVIEW: line-level PASS; the reviewer also ran the whole `dune runtest --root=tuna` (200s, rc=0). FINDINGS TAKEN: the implement pass's gate was red only because its five new cases were declared but never registered, so warnings-as-errors flagged them unused; registered in e5dfb263. The first review pass then exhausted its 1.6M input cap before writing a verdict (it had already run the full suite green), so the code-review budget rose to 3.2M input for large leaves. UNRUN: the local (offline) side still journals def rounds through repl_dict without a tree_ops row; L6 compares hashes, so the migration stays server-first as the chapter says. Divergence reporting by name is specified here but exercised only by L6's acceptance.|) *)
   (execution
    (scope l5)
    (kind code)
    (depends "spec.tuna.borg.federation#federation/dictionary-visibility"))
   (details
    (doc (|
      Definitions stop being a mutable table and become ops in the M10
      tree substrate, on the server first. A def is a put of the
      defined tree's value hash at the path dict/<identity>/<name> in
      tree_ops; repl_dict becomes a derived index of that log, like
      tree_paths. History, chaining and rewind come with the substrate.
      tree_ops versions stay local and carry no meaning across
      instances; the shared fact is the value hash, as federation
      already says.

      UNDEF is a put of the undef marker: the zero-length BYTES value,
      hash e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855.
      It is not a tree, so no def can collide with it (the leaf is
      false, nil and zero, and (def x 0) must stay a def). The substrate
      delete op is never used on dict/: apply replicates only rows that
      carry a value hash, so a delete would not reach a peer. A window
      that contains an undef ships the marker value inline like any
      other value.

      WRITE AUTHORITY. dict/ is reserved for the REPL host: grant
      minting refuses a tree grant whose path prefix covers dict/
      (including the empty prefix and partial prefixes such as d), so a
      prim run can never rewrite a dictionary; only def/undef rounds
      (and, under ns/<peer>/, fed apply) write there.

      DIVERGENCE, NOT CONFLICT. Values are immutable, so only a name
      can diverge: one name bound to different hashes in two places.
      Under federation's law it is never refused, merged or silently
      resolved. It is found by comparison: for each name, the local
      dictionary and its synced copy on the server are compared by
      hash, and a mismatch is reported with both hashes and the
      laptop's local prev_value_hash (L2; the server keeps none
      across apply).

      L5 lands together with the dictionary-visibility read filter (L6),
      never before it, so no release serves dictionaries publicly by
      default.

      ACCEPTANCE: after migration every identity's effective dictionary
      is unchanged; the dictionary at any past op is a fold of the log;
      a def round appends exactly one tree_ops row at its dict path;
      another identity's ops pull omits a private dictionary's rows and
      still verifies, and includes them once that identity sets its
      dictionary public. |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "L5: after migration every identity's effective dictionary is unchanged; the dictionary at any past op is a fold of the log; a def round appends exactly one tree_ops row at its dict path; another identity's ops pull omits a private dictionary's rows and still verifies, and includes them once that identity sets its dictionary public"))
  )
  (subsection l6-local-peer
   (status planned)
   (execution
    (scope l6)
    (kind code)
    (depends "spec.tuna.borg.local#local/l5-defs-as-ops"))
   (details
    (doc (|
      A local instance syncs as a federation peer over the existing
      /api/fed routes and adds none (LO2 otherwise).

      ENROLMENT. Peers are bearer identities the operator lists in
      TUNA_FED_PEERS; there is no self-enrolment. Each laptop is set up
      once by the operator and holds two tokens in one local file
      (mode 600), never in the dictionary or a journal: its peer token
      (push) and its owner's bearer (pull of a private dictionary, see
      below). The owner's bearer is a full credential; the owner should
      be a non-admin identity, and a read-only token is preferred if
      one exists.

      PULL: std and any value by hash (rehashed on receipt), and the
      ops window of the user's server dictionary (prefix
      dict/<identity>/). The window is seq-paged over the global log
      (5000 rows per page), so a pull walks pages from the last seq it
      saw.

      DICTIONARY VISIBILITY (owner decision 2026-11-25: private by
      default, public as an option). GET /api/fed/ops filters by prefix
      but checks no ownership, while a dictionary is private to its
      identity today (Repl_cmd.do_dict reads only the caller's rows).
      Moving dictionaries into tree_ops (L5) must not widen that, so
      the ops window gains a read filter, a deliberate federation
      change recorded under LO2 and specified in federation's chapter:

        - A row whose path is dict/<identity>/... or
          ns/<peer>/dict/<identity>/... is served only to that
          identity, to admin, or to anyone when that identity's
          dictionary visibility is public. Every other row is served
          as today.
        - Filtered rows are left out of the window. Federation already
          verifies prefix-filtered, non-contiguous windows row by row
          (each row carries its true prev_hash), so verification is
          unchanged; the page's last_seq still advances past them.
        - Visibility is a per-identity setting, private by default,
          changed only by that identity (or admin) through a REPL
          command (dict-visibility public|private) that appends a
          journaled op, so a change is auditable and rewindable.

      PRIVATE MEANS NAMES, NOT TREES. Values stay hash-gated reads
      (byte-values law 2): anyone holding a hash can fetch its tree. A
      private dictionary hides which names an identity binds to which
      hashes, and its history; it does not hide a tree whose hash has
      leaked. One more residue: a served row's prev_hash is the op_hash
      of the row before it in the global log, which may be a hidden
      dict row; someone who can guess that row's every field (seq,
      actor, path, ts, value) can confirm the guess. Accepted: it
      confirms a guess, it does not reveal one. The chapter says so
      rather than implying more.

      Because the laptop peer is its own identity (fed-peer-<name>),
      it pulls its owner's private dictionary with the owner's bearer,
      held in the same local token file; it pushes with its peer
      bearer, so writes stay confined to ns/<laptop>/.

      PUSH: one apply of the laptop's ops window into ns/<laptop>/,
      carrying its values inline. Dictionary ops land at
      ns/<laptop>/dict/<identity>/<name>. Deriv records of local runs
      travel as bytes values at ns/<laptop>/runs/<run-id>: the local
      host writes a put op at runs/<run-id> citing the record's hash
      when it seals the record, so the path arrives through the same
      window. A record above the store's value size cap (a 413 on
      apply) is not pushed. A 413 refuses the whole apply before
      anything lands, so the tool resends the window without that put
      row (rows verify one by one, so dropping one is safe); the record
      stays local and the push reports it by run id. The
      server stores records as values and does not claim to have
      executed or verified them. Anyone who reads one
      checks it with deriv-check (or server replay for fast* until L3's
      registry step lands).

      DIVERGENCE: after a push or pull, the tool compares
      ns/<laptop>/dict/... with the user's server dictionary name by
      name and reports every mismatch with both hashes (L5). Adopting
      one side is an explicit user act: a def on the side that should
      change, which is itself an ordinary op.

      CONSENT. A push writes shared state, so the local tool's push
      command asks on its terminal before each apply unless the owner
      configures a standing approval for that laptop. That prompt is
      guidance at the client, not enforcement; the enforcement is the
      server's per-peer authz (a peer writes only ns/<peer>/...).

      The divergence report's prev_value_hash is the laptop's own (L2);
      the server side reports only its current hash, since apply does
      not carry it.

      ACCEPTANCE: an offline def and run on a laptop, then push: the
      def appears at ns/<laptop>/dict/... with the same hash, the
      run's deriv record fetched back from the server verifies with
      deriv-check, and a different def of the same name made on the
      server meanwhile is reported with both hashes, neither
      overwritten. |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "L6: an offline def and run pushed via fed apply land under ns/<laptop>/ at the same hashes, the deriv record fetched back verifies with deriv-check, and a concurrent server def of the same name is reported with both hashes, neither overwritten"))
  )
  (subsection failure-definitions
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      LO1 DRIFT. A source compiles to different hashes in the CLI and
          on a manifest-seeded server, or a local run's step count
          differs from its server replay. THEN: a finding in
          FINDINGS.md; local and server share one compiler and one
          core, so drift means a front end diverged, and it is fixed
          there.
      LO2 PROTOCOL GROWTH. L6 needs a route or op kind federation
          does not have (for example server-side verification of
          pushed deriv records, or a refusing apply). THEN: record
          why, and extend federation's chapter, not this one. The
          dictionary-visibility read filter (L6) is a KNOWN, owner-
          approved instance of LO2: it is specified in federation's
          chapter before L5 lands.
      LO3 MERGE CREEP. Sync needs anything beyond hash comparison and
          two namespaces (a three-way merge, last-writer-wins, silent
          resolution). THEN: the thesis is refuted; stop and redesign
          with the owner. |))))
 )
 (verify
  (test "L1: (not true) and (list-length [1 2 3]) compile through the CLI; every corpus source compiles to the hash POST /api/programs answers for an identity with no defs on a manifest-seeded instance (tests/local_tests.ml offline; scripts/verify-18-local.sh live)")
  (test "L2: a def/eval/undef transcript gives identical result hashes and step counts in tuna repl and /api/repl for a fresh identity (scripts/verify-18-local.sh 18.2; tests/local_tests.ml l2 offline)")
  (test "L3: a local v0/v1 run with prims emits a deriv record deriv-check verifies (exit 0); a flipped journal answer exits 1; server replay reproduces result hash and steps; the same holds for fast1-fast3; a repl eval round answers a granted prim and the server's denial text for an ungranted one, sealing no record")
  (test "L4: the gate verdict through the CLI with a JSON observation answers the same JSON as /api/tool/run")
  (test "L5: after migration every identity's effective dictionary is unchanged, a past dictionary is a fold of the op log, and a def appends one tree_ops row at its dict path")
  (test "L5/L6: another identity's ops pull omits a private dictionary's rows and still verifies; after dict-visibility public the same pull includes them")
  (test "L6: an offline def and run pushed via fed apply land under ns/<laptop>/ at the same hashes, the deriv record fetched back verifies with deriv-check, and a concurrent server def of the same name is reported with both hashes, neither overwritten")))
